        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Makes a Cordless Vacuum Good on Hardwood Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best cordless vacuum for hardwood floors is not simply the one with the most suction. Bare floors need a vacuum that picks up fine dust and hair without scattering it, scratching the finish or blowing dust back into the air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Brushroll: Gentle on Hard Floors</h3>

          <p>
            A stiff bristle brushroll spinning at full speed can flick grit and debris across a bare floor before the suction catches it. Vacuums that slow the brushroll on hard floors, or let you change heads, handle hardwood better. The Shark Detect Pro and PowerPro Reveal Plus sense the floor type and adjust brushroll speed automatically.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Seeing the Dust</h3>

          <p>
            Fine dust on hardwood is hard to see under overhead light. Low headlights, like those on the Shark, Levoit and Eureka models, show it at floor level. The Dyson V12 Detect Slim uses an angled laser that the listing says makes invisible dust visible on hard floors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealed Filtration</h3>

          <p>
            On carpet, dust settles into the pile. On hardwood it sits on the surface and gets pushed back into the air easily. A sealed HEPA system, like Shark's anti-allergen complete seal, keeps what the vacuum picks up inside it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Edges and Corners</h3>

          <p>
            Dust and pet hair collect against baseboards on hard floors. Edge detection, as on the Shark Detect Pro, or a head designed to clean close to baseboards, as on the Tineco Floor ONE S5, helps.
          </p>
        </div>

