        <div id="scratches" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Can a Vacuum Scratch Hardwood Floors?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It can, though the vacuum is rarely the main cause. Knowing where scratches come from helps you avoid them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grit Is the Real Culprit</h3>

          <p>
            Sand and grit tracked in on shoes act like sandpaper underfoot. Vacuuming often is one of the best ways to protect a hardwood finish, because it removes grit before it gets ground in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Head Before Every Use</h3>

          <p>
            A small stone stuck under a vacuum head can drag across the floor. Turn the head over now and then and check the wheels and the brushroll for trapped debris.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brushroll Speed and Wheels</h3>

          <p>
            Fast-spinning stiff bristles on bare floors scatter debris and can mark soft finishes. Floor-sensing vacuums slow the brushroll on hard floors. Keep the wheels clean, since hair and grit wrapped around an axle can stop a wheel turning and make it drag.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wet-cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Can You Use a Wet-Dry Vacuum on Hardwood?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On sealed hardwood, yes, with care. On unsealed or worn floors, no.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealed vs Unsealed Floors</h3>

          <p>
            Most modern hardwood has a polyurethane or factory finish that resists small amounts of water. Older floors finished with oil or wax, and floors where the finish has worn through, can absorb water, which leads to cupping and staining. If water darkens the wood instead of beading, keep wet cleaning away. A worn floor is due for a fresh topcoat, and our guide to the best <a href="/best-water-based-polyurethane-for-floors/">water based polyurethane</a> for floors covers low-odor options you can apply yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use as Little Water as the Job Needs</h3>

          <p>
            Wet-dry vacuums like the Tineco Floor ONE S5 put down water and suck it back up in the same pass, which is far gentler than a soaking mop. Use the lowest water setting that cleans the mess, and do not leave standing water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Your Flooring Warranty</h3>

          <p>
            Some flooring makers specify which cleaners and devices are allowed. Check before you buy a wet-dry vacuum, because using a device they exclude can void the warranty. Our guide to <a href="/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/">vinyl plank flooring</a> covers a more water-tolerant alternative.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pet-hair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hardwood Floors and Pet Hair
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pet hair is easier to see on hardwood than on carpet, and easier to scatter. A few features make a real difference.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Anti-Tangle Brushrolls</h3>

          <p>
            Long pet hair and human hair wrap around ordinary brushrolls, and a wrapped roll leaves streaks of debris on bare floors. The Shark, Dyson V8, Levoit and Eureka all list self-cleaning or anti-tangle designs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Edges, Corners and Under Furniture</h3>

          <p>
            Hair drifts into corners and under sofas. A wand that lies flat, like the Levoit's, and edge detection, like the Shark Detect Pro's, reach the places hair collects.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Little and Often</h3>

          <p>
            With shedding pets, a two-minute pass every day with a light stick like the Eureka keeps hardwood cleaner than one long weekly vacuum, because hair does not have time to drift.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rugs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Hardwood With Area Rugs: One Vacuum for Both
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most hardwood homes have a rug or two. That changes which vacuum makes sense.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Automatic Floor Sensing</h3>

          <p>
            The Shark Detect Pro and PowerPro Reveal Plus detect carpet and speed the brushroll up, then slow it again on hardwood. You never switch heads, which makes mixed floors easy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rug Edges and Fringes</h3>

          <p>
            Fringes get sucked into brushrolls. Vacuum across a fringed rug edge rather than along it, or use a lower power mode, to avoid pulling threads.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Rugs Are Large or Thick</h3>

          <p>
            Deep-pile and wall-to-wall carpet need more power and runtime than hardwood. If carpet is a big part of your home, our guide to the <a href="/best-cordless-vacuum/">best cordless vacuums</a> covers stronger all-round models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Vacuum Hardwood Floors Without Scattering Dust
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good routine matters as much as the vacuum.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Use a Low Power Mode</h3>

          <p>
            Eco or standard mode is usually enough on bare floors and gives you the longest runtime. Save Boost for rugs and stubborn spots.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Work With the Grain</h3>

          <p>
            Vacuuming along the length of the boards picks up debris from the seams between them. Go slowly enough for the suction to catch fine dust.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Do the Edges With a Tool</h3>

          <p>
            Use a crevice tool along baseboards and between furniture legs, where floor heads cannot reach.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Empty the Bin and Clean the Filter</h3>

          <p>
            A full bin and clogged filter cut suction fast. Empty the bin after each session, and clean or replace the filter on the schedule the maker gives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Runtime: How Much Battery Does a Hardwood Home Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hardwood is easier on batteries than carpet, but stated runtimes need reading carefully.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the Footnotes</h3>

          <p>
            Makers often quote the best case. Shark's 40 minutes for the PowerPro is measured at the handheld in standard mode, and Levoit lists 50 minutes with a small tool but 30 with the floor head and 12 in Turbo. Expect the floor-head figure in daily use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Different Homes Need</h3>

          <p>
            An apartment or small house of mostly hardwood is usually covered by 20 to 30 minutes in a low mode. Larger homes, or homes with several rugs, benefit from 40 minutes or more, or a spare battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Charging and Storage</h3>

          <p>
            Wall docks, like the Dyson's, and freestanding storage, like the Shark Detect Pro's wand clip, keep the vacuum charged and within reach, which is what makes daily quick passes happen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-robot" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Cordless Stick vs Robot Vacuum for Hardwood Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hardwood is the floor robot vacuums handle best, so it is fair to ask whether you need a stick at all. For most homes the answer is that the two do different jobs, and many hardwood households end up with both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Robot Wins</h3>

          <p>
            A robot runs on a schedule while you are out, which means grit and pet hair are picked up every day without anyone thinking about it. On open hardwood with few obstacles, that daily pass keeps floors cleaner between proper cleans than most people manage by hand. Our guide to the <a href="/best-robot-vacuum/">best robot vacuums</a> compares mapping, suction and self-emptying docks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Stick Wins</h3>

          <p>
            A cordless stick goes where a robot cannot: stairs, the tops of baseboards, under low furniture a robot will not fit beneath, cobwebs, car seats and the crumbs under a dining table straight after a meal. It also cleans a spill the moment it happens. Every stick on this list converts to a handheld or comes with crevice and upholstery tools for exactly these jobs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Can Only Buy One</h3>

          <p>
            Choose a stick if your home has stairs, lots of furniture or rooms a robot cannot reach, or if you prefer to see and control the clean. Choose a robot if your hardwood is open-plan, you have shedding pets and you mainly want daily maintenance. Either way, keep a crevice tool or handheld for the edges, because no floor head reaches right into the corner where boards meet the baseboard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Boost on Bare Floors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boost drains the battery fast and is rarely needed on hardwood. On the Levoit it cuts runtime to 12 minutes and on the Eureka to about 15, per the listings. Use Eco or standard on bare floors and save Boost for rugs and stubborn spots.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Grit Trapped Under the Head</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single stone caught under the head or in a wheel can drag across the floor and leave a scratch. Turn the vacuum over and check the head, wheels and brushroll regularly, especially after vacuuming an entryway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Wet-Dry Vacuum on Unsealed Wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water damages unsealed, waxed or worn finishes. If water darkens the wood instead of beading on the surface, keep wet cleaning off it. Wet-dry vacuums are for sealed hardwood only, at the lowest water setting that works.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Filter Maintenance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clogged filter cuts suction and can let fine dust back into the air. Levoit says to replace its filter every three months and Tineco to clean its filter monthly. Dyson's V8 filter is washable for life, but it still needs washing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging Runtime by the Biggest Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stated runtimes are often measured with a small tool or at the handheld. Plan around the floor-head figure in a low mode, which for most picks here is well below the headline number.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

